why 11 3/4 DIRECT The soprano leaps up to B while the bass steps up to B: hidden octaves going into the dominant of the cadence. The bass moves by step, which softens them.
why 4 3/4 RANGE The new phrase starts with all voices high, the tenor on G♯ and the bass on C♯, an octave above where they just were. Bach lifts the whole chord for a brighter start and brings it down over the next beats.
why 5 0 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices step down from that high start, the bass leaping down a fifth. The phrase settles back into its normal register.
why 5 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano (B–C♯) and tenor (D♯–C♯) meet on C♯ from opposite sides by step.
why 6 0 PROGRESSION Over the bass's held E the alto and tenor move A–G♯ and C♯–B: a neighbor ⁶₄ that settles onto E major. Over a held bass this is a decoration, not a step backward.
why 7 1/2 UNRESOLVED_NCT The alto strikes G♯ again over the F♯ minor chord and falls to F♯: a suspension struck anew instead of tied.
why 8 0 RANGE The bass climbs A–B–C♯ to the fermata on C♯ major, staying high and close to the tenor; the next phrase starts lower.
why 9 1/2 LEADING_TONE The bass's D♯ leaps up to G♯, the third of the E chord, instead of rising to E. The soprano already has E, so the bass takes the third.
why 9 1/2 LEAPS After leaping down to D♯ the bass leaps back up to G♯ instead of stepping: it outlines V⁶ and then I⁶, moving with the harmony.
why 9 3/4 DOUBLING Soprano and tenor meet on D♯ by step. In this chromatic passage both move on down, so the leading tone is a decoration here, not a goal.
why 10 0 LEADING_TONE Neither D♯ rises: the soprano's falls to C♯ because that is the hymn tune, and the tenor's drops to B and G♯. The music is turning toward F♯, so E is not the goal here.
why 12 0 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Tenor and bass go from F♯/B to B/E a fifth apart in the final cadence, but the tenor moves first, an eighth early. Staggered like this Bach accepts them; strict exercises still count them.
